ML_PAGE("t4-notes", "<header class=\"page-header theme-l4\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">TOPIC 4 · COMPLETE REFERENCE</span><h2>Full Intuitive Explanation</h2><p>The complete Topic 4 narrative, preserved in full. Use the guided lessons for a first pass; open this when a concept still isn't clicking.</p></header><div class=\"action-row\"><button class=\"primary-btn bg-l4\" data-action=\"print\">Print this page</button><button class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"t4-subjective\">Go to subjective prep</button><button class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"t4-prep\">Go to MCQ prep</button></div><details class=\"topic-index toc-panel theme-l4\" id=\"t4n-toc\"><summary><strong>Reading order</strong><span class=\"toc-count\">63 sections</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><input type=\"search\" class=\"toc-search\" placeholder=\"Filter sections...\" aria-label=\"Filter sections\"><ul class=\"toc-list\"><li data-full=\"1. Sustainability is fundamentally a time-horizon problem\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4n-s1\" title=\"1. Sustainability is fundamentally a time-horizon problem\"><span class=\"toc-no\">1</span>Sustainability as time horizon</button></li><li data-full=\"2. Sustainability is not the same thing as environmentalism\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4n-s2\" title=\"2. Sustainability is not the same thing as environmentalism\"><span class=\"toc-no\">2</span>Not just environmentalism</button></li><li data-full=\"3. The evolution of sustainability shows how the concept became progressively broader\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4n-s3\" title=\"3. The evolution of sustainability shows how the concept became progressively broader\"><span class=\"toc-no\">3</span>Evolution of sustainability</button></li><li data-full=\"4. Triple Bottom Line: one of the most important concepts\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4n-s4\" title=\"4. Triple Bottom Line: one of the most important concepts\"><span class=\"toc-no\">4</span>Triple Bottom Line</button></li><li data-full=\"5. The hidden issue: externalised costs\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4n-s5\" title=\"5. The hidden issue: externalised costs\"><span class=\"toc-no\">5</span>Externalised costs</button></li><li data-full=\"6. The SDGs represent another major expansion\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4n-s6\" title=\"6. The SDGs represent another major expansion\"><span class=\"toc-no\">6</span>SDGs and the 5Ps</button></li><li data-full=\"7. Don&#x27;t memorise the 17 SDGs as 17 unrelated coloured squares\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4n-s7\" title=\"7. Don&#x27;t memorise the 17 SDGs as 17 unrelated coloured squares\"><span class=\"toc-no\">7</span>SDGs as biosphere-society-economy</button></li><li data-full=\"8. SDGs are global goals; ESG is a corporate/investment lens\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4n-s8\" title=\"8. SDGs are global goals; ESG is a corporate/investment lens\"><span class=\"toc-no\">8</span>SDGs vs ESG</button></li><li data-full=\"9. Corporate sustainability translates the societal concept into business strategy\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4n-s9\" title=\"9. Corporate sustainability translates the societal concept into business strategy\"><span class=\"toc-no\">9</span>Corporate sustainability</button></li><li data-full=\"10. CSR and corporate sustainability should not be mentally collapsed\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4n-s10\" title=\"10. CSR and corporate sustainability should not be mentally collapsed\"><span class=\"toc-no\">10</span>CSR vs corporate sustainability</button></li><li data-full=\"11. Reporting standards exist because vague sustainability claims are almost useless\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4n-s11\" title=\"11. Reporting standards exist because vague sustainability claims are almost useless\"><span class=\"toc-no\">11</span>Why reporting standards exist</button></li><li data-full=\"12. ESG information extends the information set beyond conventional financial statements\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4n-s12\" title=\"12. ESG information extends the information set beyond conventional financial statements\"><span class=\"toc-no\">12</span>ESG beyond financial statements</button></li><li data-full=\"13. What exactly is ESG?\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4n-s13\" title=\"13. What exactly is ESG?\"><span class=\"toc-no\">13</span>What exactly is ESG?</button></li><li data-full=\"14. Environmental risk works in both directions\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4n-s14\" title=\"14. Environmental risk works in both directions\"><span class=\"toc-no\">14</span>Environmental risk both ways</button></li><li data-full=\"15. Environmental risk can become conventional financial risk\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4n-s15\" title=\"15. Environmental risk can become conventional financial risk\"><span class=\"toc-no\">15</span>E risk becomes financial risk</button></li><li data-full=\"16. Social - the human side of the enterprise\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4n-s16\" title=\"16. Social - the human side of the enterprise\"><span class=\"toc-no\">16</span>Social</button></li><li data-full=\"17. Social risk is not simply &quot;being nice to employees&quot;\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4n-s17\" title=\"17. Social risk is not simply &quot;being nice to employees&quot;\"><span class=\"toc-no\">17</span>Social risk transmits</button></li><li data-full=\"18. Governance - how the organisation directs itself\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4n-s18\" title=\"18. Governance - how the organisation directs itself\"><span class=\"toc-no\">18</span>Governance</button></li><li data-full=\"19. Governance is the mechanism that makes E and S credible\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4n-s19\" title=\"19. Governance is the mechanism that makes E and S credible\"><span class=\"toc-no\">19</span>G makes E and S credible</button></li><li data-full=\"20. Why ESG became important to capital markets\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4n-s20\" title=\"20. Why ESG became important to capital markets\"><span class=\"toc-no\">20</span>ESG and capital markets</button></li><li data-full=\"21. ESG is important because multiple stakeholders influence enterprise value\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4n-s21\" title=\"21. ESG is important because multiple stakeholders influence enterprise value\"><span class=\"toc-no\">21</span>Stakeholders and value</button></li><li data-full=\"22. ESG itself should not become the objective\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4n-s22\" title=\"22. ESG itself should not become the objective\"><span class=\"toc-no\">22</span>ESG is not the objective</button></li><li data-full=\"23. What is ESG risk?\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4n-s23\" title=\"23. What is ESG risk?\"><span class=\"toc-no\">23</span>What is ESG risk?</button></li><li data-full=\"24. Materiality prevents ESG from becoming an infinite checklist\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4n-s24\" title=\"24. Materiality prevents ESG from becoming an infinite checklist\"><span class=\"toc-no\">24</span>Materiality</button></li><li data-full=\"25. ESG risks are usually risk drivers, not isolated final consequences\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4n-s25\" title=\"25. ESG risks are usually risk drivers, not isolated final consequences\"><span class=\"toc-no\">25</span>ESG risks are drivers</button></li><li data-full=\"26. A useful ESG risk chain\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4n-s26\" title=\"26. A useful ESG risk chain\"><span class=\"toc-no\">26</span>A useful ESG risk chain</button></li><li data-full=\"27. The cases on page 31 are not random scandals - each demonstrates a transmission mechanism\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4n-s27\" title=\"27. The cases on page 31 are not random scandals - each demonstrates a transmission mechanism\"><span class=\"toc-no\">27</span>The ESG cases on the slides</button></li><li data-full=\"28. Greenwashing: one of the most important concepts in this topic\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4n-s28\" title=\"28. Greenwashing: one of the most important concepts in this topic\"><span class=\"toc-no\">28</span>Greenwashing</button></li><li data-full=\"29. Why greenwashing is a governance problem as much as an environmental problem\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4n-s29\" title=\"29. Why greenwashing is a governance problem as much as an environmental problem\"><span class=\"toc-no\">29</span>Greenwashing is a G problem</button></li><li data-full=\"30. The Volkswagen example shows how environmental misconduct becomes financial loss\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4n-s30\" title=\"30. The Volkswagen example shows how environmental misconduct becomes financial loss\"><span class=\"toc-no\">30</span>Volkswagen</button></li><li data-full=\"31. Tlou Energy illustrates another greenwashing pathway\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4n-s31\" title=\"31. Tlou Energy illustrates another greenwashing pathway\"><span class=\"toc-no\">31</span>Tlou Energy</button></li><li data-full=\"32. Coca-Cola example: ESG claims can be challenged against operational reality\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4n-s32\" title=\"32. Coca-Cola example: ESG claims can be challenged against operational reality\"><span class=\"toc-no\">32</span>Coca-Cola</button></li><li data-full=\"33. What does ESG risk management actually do?\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4n-s33\" title=\"33. What does ESG risk management actually do?\"><span class=\"toc-no\">33</span>What ESG RM does</button></li><li data-full=\"34. ESG risk management should therefore follow a familiar ERM cycle\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4n-s34\" title=\"34. ESG risk management should therefore follow a familiar ERM cycle\"><span class=\"toc-no\">34</span>ESG RM follows the ERM cycle</button></li><li data-full=\"35. The value of ESG risk management is largely about earlier detection\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4n-s35\" title=\"35. The value of ESG risk management is largely about earlier detection\"><span class=\"toc-no\">35</span>Early detection</button></li><li data-full=\"36. Think in terms of ESG KRIs\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4n-s36\" title=\"36. Think in terms of ESG KRIs\"><span class=\"toc-no\">36</span>Think in terms of ESG KRIs</button></li><li data-full=\"37. Sustainability reporting and ESG risk management are related but not identical\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4n-s37\" title=\"37. Sustainability reporting and ESG risk management are related but not identical\"><span class=\"toc-no\">37</span>Reporting vs risk management</button></li><li data-full=\"38. This explains why sustainability reporting requires controls\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4n-s38\" title=\"38. This explains why sustainability reporting requires controls\"><span class=\"toc-no\">38</span>Reporting needs controls</button></li><li data-full=\"39. Green finance vs green investing\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4n-s39\" title=\"39. Green finance vs green investing\"><span class=\"toc-no\">39</span>Green finance vs green investing</button></li><li data-full=\"40. Sustainable finance creates an important incentive mechanism\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4n-s40\" title=\"40. Sustainable finance creates an important incentive mechanism\"><span class=\"toc-no\">40</span>Sustainable finance incentives</button></li><li data-full=\"41. Malaysia&#x27;s sustainability context\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4n-s41\" title=\"41. Malaysia&#x27;s sustainability context\"><span class=\"toc-no\">41</span>Malaysia and net zero</button></li><li data-full=\"42. Corporate examples show that firms map sustainability differently\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4n-s42\" title=\"42. Corporate examples show that firms map sustainability differently\"><span class=\"toc-no\">42</span>IOI and PETRONAS examples</button></li><li data-full=\"43. This is why copying another company&#x27;s ESG strategy is weak\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4n-s43\" title=\"43. This is why copying another company&#x27;s ESG strategy is weak\"><span class=\"toc-no\">43</span>Don't copy ESG strategies</button></li><li data-full=\"44. Malaysia&#x27;s ESG practices\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4n-s44\" title=\"44. Malaysia&#x27;s ESG practices\"><span class=\"toc-no\">44</span>Malaysia's ESG practices</button></li><li data-full=\"45. FTSE4Good Bursa Malaysia: don&#x27;t misunderstand what an ESG index is doing\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4n-s45\" title=\"45. FTSE4Good Bursa Malaysia: don&#x27;t misunderstand what an ESG index is doing\"><span class=\"toc-no\">45</span>FTSE4Good Bursa Malaysia</button></li><li data-full=\"46. Sustainability vs ESG metrics - probably one of the most examinable distinctions\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4n-s46\" title=\"46. Sustainability vs ESG metrics - probably one of the most examinable distinctions\"><span class=\"toc-no\">46</span>Sustainability vs ESG metrics</button></li><li data-full=\"47. A thermometer analogy\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4n-s47\" title=\"47. A thermometer analogy\"><span class=\"toc-no\">47</span>A thermometer analogy</button></li><li data-full=\"48. The chapter&#x27;s ASEAN comparison should be interpreted cautiously\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4n-s48\" title=\"48. The chapter&#x27;s ASEAN comparison should be interpreted cautiously\"><span class=\"toc-no\">48</span>ASEAN comparison</button></li><li data-full=\"49. The sustainable bond and sukuk chart is really about capital-market response\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4n-s49\" title=\"49. The sustainable bond and sukuk chart is really about capital-market response\"><span class=\"toc-no\">49</span>Sustainable bonds and sukuk</button></li><li data-full=\"50. A difficult integrated case\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4n-s50\" title=\"50. A difficult integrated case\"><span class=\"toc-no\">50</span>Case: manufacturer seeking FTSE4Good</button></li><li data-full=\"51. Start with governance, not marketing\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4n-s51\" title=\"51. Start with governance, not marketing\"><span class=\"toc-no\">51</span>Start with governance, not marketing</button></li><li data-full=\"52. Then determine material ESG risks\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4n-s52\" title=\"52. Then determine material ESG risks\"><span class=\"toc-no\">52</span>Then determine material ESG risks</button></li><li data-full=\"53. Establish baselines\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4n-s53\" title=\"53. Establish baselines\"><span class=\"toc-no\">53</span>Establish baselines</button></li><li data-full=\"54. Assess suppliers\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4n-s54\" title=\"54. Assess suppliers\"><span class=\"toc-no\">54</span>Assess suppliers</button></li><li data-full=\"55. Create controls and KRIs\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4n-s55\" title=\"55. Create controls and KRIs\"><span class=\"toc-no\">55</span>Create controls and KRIs</button></li><li data-full=\"56. Only then report\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4n-s56\" title=\"56. Only then report\"><span class=\"toc-no\">56</span>Only then report</button></li><li data-full=\"57. Why ESG index inclusion should be an outcome\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4n-s57\" title=\"57. Why ESG index inclusion should be an outcome\"><span class=\"toc-no\">57</span>Index inclusion is an outcome</button></li><li data-full=\"58. A second difficult case\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4n-s58\" title=\"58. A second difficult case\"><span class=\"toc-no\">58</span>Case: carbon-neutral pledge</button></li><li data-full=\"59. Evaluate whether the carbon-neutral pledge itself is good\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4n-s59\" title=\"59. Evaluate whether the carbon-neutral pledge itself is good\"><span class=\"toc-no\">59</span>Evaluate the pledge</button></li><li data-full=\"60. Bloom&#x27;s Taxonomy applied to Topic 4\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4n-s60\" title=\"60. Bloom&#x27;s Taxonomy applied to Topic 4\"><span class=\"toc-no\">60</span>Bloom's levels for Topic 4</button></li><li data-full=\"61. The most important distinctions to master\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4n-s61\" title=\"61. The most important distinctions to master\"><span class=\"toc-no\">61</span>Key distinctions table</button></li><li data-full=\"62. How Topic 4 connects all four chapters\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4n-s62\" title=\"62. How Topic 4 connects all four chapters\"><span class=\"toc-no\">62</span>How the four topics connect</button></li><li data-full=\"63. The deepest lesson from Topic 4\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t4n-s63\" title=\"63. The deepest lesson from Topic 4\"><span class=\"toc-no\">63</span>The deepest lesson from Topic 4</button></li></ul></details><details class=\"full-notes theme-l4\" open><summary><div><strong>Topic 4 - complete intuitive explanation</strong><small>Every section of the notes, with worked examples and interactive visuals.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"markdown-body\"><div class=\"notes-toolbar\"><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"open\">Expand all</button><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"close\">Collapse all</button><span>63 sections · click a heading to open it</span></div><p>Topic 4 is where the course changes perspective again.</p>\n<p>Topic 1 taught you <strong>what risk is</strong>. Topic 2 taught you <strong>why organisations need enterprise-wide risk management</strong>. Topic 3 taught you <strong>how governance, standards and regulation make risk management accountable</strong>. Topic 4 now asks something broader:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Can the enterprise create value today without destroying the conditions needed to create value tomorrow?</span></div>\n<p>That is the heart of <strong>sustainability risk management</strong>.</p>\n<p>The chapter covers sustainability, the UN Sustainable Development Goals and Environmental, Social and Governance practices. It then connects these ideas to corporate sustainability, reporting standards, ESG risk management and the Malaysian ESG landscape.</p>\n<p>The most useful mental model for the entire chapter is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow boxed\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Sustainability → SDGs → Corporate Sustainability → ESG → ESG Metrics → ESG Risks → Risk Management → Long-term Value\"><span>Sustainability</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>SDGs</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Corporate Sustainability</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>ESG</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>ESG Metrics</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>ESG Risks</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Risk Management</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Long-term Value</span></div>\n<p>Once you understand the differences between those concepts, this chapter becomes much easier.</p>\n<hr /><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4n-s1\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">1. Sustainability is fundamentally a time-horizon problem</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"t4_time_horizon_sim\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Cutting more, earning less</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Choose how much forest to cut each year; compare year-one profit with the 20-year total.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Your slides define sustainability as meeting the needs of the current generation without compromising the needs of future generations, while balancing economic growth, environmental care and social well-being.</p>\n<p>The phrase <strong>future generations</strong> is the important part.</p>\n<p>Suppose a logging company can earn:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$RM500\\text{ million}$$</div>\n<p>this year by cutting down almost every tree it owns.</p>\n<p>From a narrow one-year accounting perspective:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Profit} \\uparrow$$</div>\n<p>Fantastic.</p>\n<p>But if there is no commercially usable forest next year:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Future productive capacity} \\downarrow$$</div>\n<p>The decision may therefore maximise:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$V_{today}$$</div>\n<p>while destroying:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$V_{future}$$</div>\n<p>Sustainability asks whether the activity can continue without consuming the environmental, social or economic foundations on which the activity itself depends.</p>\n<p>So a better intuitive definition is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Sustainability}\n=\n\\text{meeting present needs without destroying future capacity}\n}$$</div>\n<p>That is much deeper than simply thinking:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Sustainability = recycling.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4n-s2\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">2. Sustainability is not the same thing as environmentalism</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>This is the first trap.</p>\n<p>The chapter explicitly balances:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Economic</span></div>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Environmental</span></div>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Social</span></div>\n<p>dimensions.</p>\n<p>A company cannot realistically call itself sustainable simply because it plants trees while simultaneously:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{losing money every year}$$</div>\n<p>because an economically non-viable organisation will eventually disappear.</p>\n<p>Likewise, a highly profitable company using forced labour would also violate the broader sustainability concept.</p>\n<p>So sustainability asks:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Can economic prosperity coexist with environmental protection and social well-being over time?</span></div>\n<p>That balance is central to the chapter.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4n-s3\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">3. The evolution of sustainability shows how the concept became progressively broader</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Page 4 gives a timeline beginning with the <strong>1987 Brundtland Commission</strong>, moving through the 1992 UN Conference, the Triple Bottom Line, the Millennium Development Goals and finally the 2015 Sustainable Development Goals.</p>\n<p>Don't memorise the timeline as arbitrary dates.</p>\n<p>What matters is how the idea evolved.</p>\n<p>The chapter describes the 1987 Brundtland Commission as a major turning point, initially emphasising the environmental or <strong>planet</strong> dimension. The 1992 UN Conference gave greater emphasis to social actors, and the WBCSD helped bring sustainability concepts into business, linking environmental and economic concerns.</p>\n<p>Conceptually, the progression is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Environment}$$</div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Environment + Development}$$</div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single\"><span>Business + Society + Environment</span></div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Integrated Sustainable Development</span></div>\n<p>The concept expanded because real-world sustainability problems cannot be separated neatly.</p>\n<p>Climate problems affect economies.</p>\n<p>Economic inequality affects social stability.</p>\n<p>Poor governance affects environmental enforcement.</p>\n<p>Everything starts interacting.</p>\n<p>That sounds familiar because it is exactly the same logic behind <strong>ERM</strong>.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4n-s4\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">4. Triple Bottom Line: one of the most important concepts</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t4_tbl_5p\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>The success measure widened from profit to the 3Ps, then to the SDG 5Ps.</figcaption></figure></div><p>The chapter explains that John Elkington's Triple Bottom Line brought together economic, environmental and societal dimensions, often represented as the <strong>3Ps</strong>.</p>\n<p>Those are:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>People</span></div>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Planet</span></div>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Profit</span></div>\n<p>Traditional business analysis might ask:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Did the company make money?}$$</div>\n<p>Triple Bottom Line thinking asks:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single\"><span>Did it create economic value?</span></div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{What did it do to society?}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-flow single\"><span>What did it do to the environment?</span></div>\n<p>For example, imagine Factory A earns:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$RM100m \\text{ profit}$$</div>\n<p>but achieves that by:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{dumping toxic waste}$$</div>\n<p>and</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single\"><span>using dangerous labour practices</span></div>\n<p>A traditional income statement may still show:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$RM100m$$</div>\n<p>but the Triple Bottom Line perspective asks whether some of that apparent value was created by transferring costs onto:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{workers}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{community}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{environment}$$</div>\n<p>That is a much more sophisticated way of thinking about performance.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4n-s5\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">5. The hidden issue: externalised costs</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"t4_externality_slider\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Hidden costs come home</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Allow more pollution and watch reported profit rise while society pays; then let the regulator send the bill home.</figcaption></figure></div><p>This term isn't the chapter's central terminology, so treat this as an intuitive extension.</p>\n<p>Suppose pollution causes:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$RM20m$$</div>\n<p>of health and environmental damage.</p>\n<p>If the company does not pay that RM20m, its accounts may show:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Profit = RM100m$$</div>\n<p>But society experiences:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$100m-20m=RM80m$$</div>\n<p>of broader net benefit, assuming those were the only external costs.</p>\n<p>This explains why sustainability metrics attempt to reveal effects that ordinary financial statements may not fully show.</p>\n<p>The company can look financially successful while creating:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>future environmental, legal, reputational or social liabilities</span></div>\n<p>This is where sustainability becomes directly relevant to <strong>risk management</strong>.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4n-s6\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">6. The SDGs represent another major expansion</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter describes the UN's 2030 Agenda as establishing <strong>17 Sustainable Development Goals and 169 targets</strong>, organised around:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>People</span></div>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Planet</span></div>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Prosperity</span></div>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Peace</span></div>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Partnership</span></div>\n<p>or the <strong>5Ps</strong>.</p>\n<p>This is broader than the Triple Bottom Line.</p>\n<p>You can think of the evolution as:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$3Ps\n\\rightarrow\n5Ps$$</div>\n<p>Not because profit suddenly became irrelevant, but because sustainable development was being framed as an entire societal system rather than simply corporate performance.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4n-s7\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">7. Don't memorise the 17 SDGs as 17 unrelated coloured squares</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t4_wedding_cake\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>The SDG &quot;wedding cake&quot;: the economy depends on society, which depends on the biosphere.</figcaption></figure></div><p>The diagrams on pages 11-14 show all 17 goals, but page 14 contains a particularly useful visual: it stacks the SDGs into layers of <strong>biosphere → society → economy</strong>.</p>\n<p>That diagram makes an important conceptual claim:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>The economy exists inside society, and society exists inside the biosphere.</span></div>\n<p>This is much more useful than treating the economy, society and environment as three completely separate worlds.</p>\n<p>A company requires:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{people}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{water}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{energy}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{land}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{institutions}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{social stability}$$</div>\n<p>to operate.</p>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Economic activity ultimately depends on environmental and social systems.</span></div>\n<p>That is why environmental degradation can eventually become financial risk.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4n-s8\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">8. SDGs are global goals; ESG is a corporate/investment lens</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"t4_concept_sorter\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Eight things called &quot;sustainability&quot;</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Match each statement to the concept it describes, then reveal.</figcaption></figure></div><p>This distinction is extremely important.</p>\n<p>The SDGs answer roughly:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>What sustainable-development outcomes should the world pursue?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>ESG asks roughly:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>How is an organisation performing or exposed across environmental, social and governance factors?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>So:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{SDGs} = \\text{broad societal goals}}$$</div>\n<p>while:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{ESG} = \\text{organisational factors/metrics/practices}}$$</div>\n<p>They overlap, but they are not interchangeable.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<p>SDG 13 concerns climate action.</p>\n<p>A company might contribute through ESG actions such as:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{measuring GHG emissions}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{reducing energy consumption}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{renewable-energy investment}$$</div>\n<p>The chapter explicitly states that ESG practices can contribute to the SDGs and that SDG contributions can become part of the sustainability information available to stakeholders.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4n-s9\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">9. Corporate sustainability translates the societal concept into business strategy</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter defines corporate sustainability as business strategies and activities that meet the needs of the enterprise and its stakeholders today while protecting and enhancing the human and natural resources needed in the future.</p>\n<p>This means:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Sustainable Development}$$</div>\n<p>is the broader societal idea,</p>\n<p>while:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Corporate Sustainability}$$</div>\n<p>asks:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>What does that mean for a business?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This is where things become strategically interesting.</p>\n<p>Corporate sustainability is not simply:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Company donates money to charity.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>It affects:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{business model}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{supply chain}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{capital expenditure}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{products}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{human capital}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{governance}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{risk management}$$</div>\n<p>If sustainability does not influence any serious business decision, it is difficult to call it strategic.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4n-s10\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">10. CSR and corporate sustainability should not be mentally collapsed</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter doesn't build a full CSR comparison, so I won't invent one as course content. But conceptually, avoid assuming corporate sustainability merely means philanthropic activity.</p>\n<p>Imagine two palm-oil companies.</p>\n<p>Company A donates RM5 million to schools.</p>\n<p>But its supply chain contains severe sustainability problems.</p>\n<p>Company B redesigns supplier monitoring, improves traceability and changes operating practices.</p>\n<p>Which action more fundamentally changes the sustainability profile of the business?</p>\n<p>Clearly B.</p>\n<p>So the higher-order question is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Has sustainability changed the business model and risk profile?</span></div>\n<p>not:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Did the company run a nice CSR campaign?\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4n-s11\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">11. Reporting standards exist because vague sustainability claims are almost useless</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Page 8 introduces sustainability reporting standards and shows GRI, ISO, IFRS-related reporting and the International Sustainability Standards Board.</p>\n<p>Why do we need standards?</p>\n<p>Suppose Company A announces:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"We are extremely green.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Company B says:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"We are super sustainable.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Company C says:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Planet first!\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>What can an investor actually compare?</p>\n<p>Almost nothing.</p>\n<p>Reporting frameworks attempt to transform vague claims into structured information:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow boxed\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Narrative Claims → Comparable Disclosure → Better Decision Information\"><span>Narrative Claims</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Comparable Disclosure</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Better Decision Information</span></div>\n<p>This is closely related to Topic 3's discussion of financial reporting and governance.</p>\n<p>Good decisions require reliable information.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4n-s12\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">12. ESG information extends the information set beyond conventional financial statements</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Traditional financial reporting tells you things such as:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Revenue}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Expenses}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Assets}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Liabilities}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Cash flows}$$</div>\n<p>ESG information attempts to provide visibility over issues such as:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{GHG emissions}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{labour standards}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{worker safety}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{board structure}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{corruption}$$</div>\n<p>Why might investors care?</p>\n<p>Because these can eventually affect:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>future cash flows, cost, access to capital, reputation and enterprise value</span></div>\n<p>Now ESG starts looking less like ideology and more like risk information.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4n-s13\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">13. What exactly is ESG?</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter separates ESG into three groups.</p>\n<h3>Environmental - interaction with the physical environment</h3>\n<p>The slides include:</p>\n<p>energy consumption,</p>\n<p>greenhouse-gas emissions,</p>\n<p>deforestation,</p>\n<p>biodiversity,</p>\n<p>climate change,</p>\n<p>pollution mitigation,</p>\n<p>waste management,</p>\n<p>water usage.</p>\n<p>The intuitive question is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>How does the company depend on and affect the natural environment?</span></div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4n-s14\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">14. Environmental risk works in both directions</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t4_esg_two_way\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Impact (company on environment) and exposure (environment on company) are both part of ESG risk.</figcaption></figure></div><p>This is a very important analysis-level insight.</p>\n<p>People often think only:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Company → Environment\"><span>Company</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Environment</span></div>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<p>factory emits CO₂.</p>\n<p>But risk management must also consider:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Environment → Company\"><span>Environment</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Company</span></div>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<p>flood damages factory.</p>\n<p>So environmental analysis can involve:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Company&#x27;s impact on environment</span></div>\n<p>and</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Environmental conditions affecting company</span></div>\n<p>Imagine a plantation.</p>\n<p>Deforestation may create ESG concerns from the firm's activities.</p>\n<p>But drought caused by climate conditions may reduce the plantation's crop yield.</p>\n<p>Both matter.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4n-s15\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">15. Environmental risk can become conventional financial risk</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Drought}$$</div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Water shortage}$$</div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Production declines}$$</div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Revenue declines}$$</div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Cash flow deteriorates}$$</div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Liquidity pressure}$$</div>\n<p>What began as an environmental problem eventually becomes:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>financial risk</span></div>\n<p>This is why ESG should not be kept in a separate colourful sustainability PowerPoint disconnected from ERM.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4n-s16\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">16. Social - the human side of the enterprise</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter includes issues such as:</p>\n<p>labour standards,</p>\n<p>wages and benefits,</p>\n<p>diversity and inclusion,</p>\n<p>leadership,</p>\n<p>pay equity,</p>\n<p>talent management,</p>\n<p>community relations,</p>\n<p>privacy and data protection,</p>\n<p>health and safety,</p>\n<p>supply-chain management.</p>\n<p>An intuitive question is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>How does the organisation treat and depend on people?</span></div>\n<p>That includes:</p>\n<p>employees,</p>\n<p>customers,</p>\n<p>suppliers,</p>\n<p>communities.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4n-s17\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">17. Social risk is not simply \"being nice to employees\"</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Consider forced labour in the supply chain.</p>\n<p>At first glance:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Social problem}$$</div>\n<p>But then:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{forced-labour allegation}$$</div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{import restrictions}$$</div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{products blocked}$$</div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{lost revenue}$$</div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{customer/investor concern}$$</div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{reputational damage}$$</div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{financial impact}$$</div>\n<p>The chapter actually gives a related Malaysian example: products from several Malaysian glove and oil-palm companies were withheld by US Customs and Border Protection following forced-labour allegations.</p>\n<p>That is an excellent demonstration of:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\nS \\rightarrow \\text{Operational/Market/Reputation/Financial consequences}\n}$$</div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4n-s18\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">18. Governance - how the organisation directs itself</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter puts under governance:</p>\n<p>board composition and structure,</p>\n<p>strategic oversight,</p>\n<p>compliance,</p>\n<p>executive compensation,</p>\n<p>lobbying,</p>\n<p>bribery,</p>\n<p>corruption.</p>\n<p>This should feel very familiar from Topic 3.</p>\n<p>The intuitive question is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Who controls the organisation, how are they incentivised, and can they be trusted?</span></div>\n<p>Governance is especially important because weak governance can undermine the Environmental and Social components too.</p>\n<p>A company may announce:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Net zero by 2050.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>But if the board:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>does not monitor emissions,</li>\n<li>does not link responsibility to anyone,</li>\n<li>allows misleading disclosures,</li>\n</ul>\n<p>then the E target is partly a G problem.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4n-s19\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">19. Governance is the mechanism that makes E and S credible</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t4_g_backbone\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Without governance, environmental and social commitments have no owner, no verification and no accountability.</figcaption></figure></div><p>You can model it as:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\nG\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{oversight of E and S}\n}$$</div>\n<p>Suppose a company has a pollution target.</p>\n<p>Who approved it?</p>\n<p>Who measures it?</p>\n<p>Who verifies the data?</p>\n<p>Who reports progress?</p>\n<p>Who is accountable if performance fails?</p>\n<p>Those are governance questions.</p>\n<p>Therefore ESG is not three completely independent buckets.</p>\n<p>They interact.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4n-s20\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">20. Why ESG became important to capital markets</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter notes that the ESG-investing idea was promoted through a 2004 initiative associated with then-UN Secretary-General Kofi Annan encouraging major financial institutions to integrate ESG considerations into capital markets.</p>\n<p>The significant intellectual shift is:</p>\n<p>Traditional investor:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>What return will this asset produce?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>More developed risk question:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>What E, S and G factors could alter that expected return?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Weak governance → fraud risk\"><span>Weak governance</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>fraud risk</span></div>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"poor labour standards → supply disruption\"><span>poor labour standards</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>supply disruption</span></div>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"environmental regulation → higher compliance cost\"><span>environmental regulation</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>higher compliance cost</span></div>\n<p>Therefore ESG information can become relevant to valuation.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4n-s21\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">21. ESG is important because multiple stakeholders influence enterprise value</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Pages 28-29 say investors increasingly seek sustainability information, while consumers and employees may also prefer companies perceived positively on such issues.</p>\n<p>This gives us three pathways.</p>\n<p>Investor behaviour can affect:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{capital access}$$</div>\n<p>Consumer behaviour can affect:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{revenue}$$</div>\n<p>Employee behaviour can affect:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single\"><span>talent attraction + retention</span></div>\n<p>So ESG can influence both sides of corporate value:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Revenue/operations}\n+\n\\text{financing/capital}\n}$$</div>\n<p>That is why ESG can become strategically relevant.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4n-s22\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">22. ESG itself should not become the objective</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>This is a subtle but very important evaluation-level point.</p>\n<p>Suppose management says:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Our goal is to get an ESG score of 90.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That can become dangerous.</p>\n<p>Why?</p>\n<p>Because the company may start optimising the <strong>score</strong> instead of the underlying sustainability performance.</p>\n<p>A better chain is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Identify material ESG issues</span></div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Improve actual practices</span></div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Measure performance</span></div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Report credibly</span></div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Strong rating/index eligibility may follow</span></div>\n<p>The score should ideally be an <strong>outcome or signal</strong>, not the sole purpose of the programme.</p>\n<p>Otherwise the organisation risks turning sustainability into a box-ticking exercise.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4n-s23\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">23. What is ESG risk?</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Your slides define ESG risk as an environmental, social or governance event/condition that could create a material negative impact on investment value.</p>\n<p>The word I want you to notice is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Material</span></div>\n<p>Not every ESG issue is equally important to every company.</p>\n<p>For a semiconductor manufacturer:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{water availability}$$</div>\n<p>might be highly material.</p>\n<p>For a software consultancy:</p>\n<p>perhaps less so.</p>\n<p>For a bank:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single\"><span>governance + data privacy + lending exposure</span></div>\n<p>may dominate.</p>\n<p>For a plantation:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single\"><span>land use + biodiversity + labour</span></div>\n<p>may dominate.</p>\n<p>So:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>ESG risk assessment should be business-specific.</span></div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4n-s24\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">24. Materiality prevents ESG from becoming an infinite checklist</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"t4_materiality\"><div class=\"vis-title\">ESG materiality by industry</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Choose an industry; its material E, S and G issues light up. Materiality is business-specific.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Without materiality, ESG can become absurd:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Here are 600 possible sustainability indicators. Please enjoy.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Risk management requires prioritisation.</p>\n<p>Suppose a company faces:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$E_1,E_2,\\ldots,E_{50}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$S_1,S_2,\\ldots,S_{40}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$G_1,G_2,\\ldots,G_{30}$$</div>\n<p>Management cannot treat all 120 as equally important.</p>\n<p>Instead:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow boxed\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Materiality → focus on ESG factors capable of meaningfully affecting objectives/value\"><span>Materiality</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>focus on ESG factors capable of meaningfully affecting objectives/value</span></div>\n<p>That is directly consistent with ERM.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4n-s25\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">25. ESG risks are usually risk drivers, not isolated final consequences</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>This is perhaps the most important ERM connection.</p>\n<p>Suppose:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Poor working conditions}$$</div>\n<p>That is an S issue.</p>\n<p>But the consequences might include:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{employee turnover}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{production disruption}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{lawsuits}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{regulatory action}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{customer boycott}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{reputation loss}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{lower revenue}$$</div>\n<p>So:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow boxed\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"ESG Driver → Traditional Enterprise Risks → Value Impact\"><span>ESG Driver</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Traditional Enterprise Risks</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Value Impact</span></div>\n<p>That is how I would integrate Topic 4 with Topics 1-3.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4n-s26\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">26. A useful ESG risk chain</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"t4_esg_chain\"><div class=\"vis-title\">How an ESG issue transmits</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Step an ESG issue through the business until it reaches enterprise value.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Use this mentally whenever you receive a scenario:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>ESG Issue</span></div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Risk Event</span></div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Business Consequence</span></div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Financial Consequence</span></div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Stakeholder Response</span></div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Enterprise Value Impact</span></div>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Unsafe working conditions}$$</div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<p>Serious accident</p>\n<p>↓</p>\n<p>Factory shutdown</p>\n<p>↓</p>\n<p>Lost revenue</p>\n<p>↓</p>\n<p>Regulator + employees + customers react</p>\n<p>↓</p>\n<p>Reputation/cost of capital/valuation affected.</p>\n<p>That's much stronger than merely saying:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Health and safety = Social ESG.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4n-s27\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">27. The cases on page 31 are not random scandals - each demonstrates a transmission mechanism</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter presents several prominent ESG cases.</p>\n<p>Consider what each is teaching.</p>\n<p><strong>PETRONAS:</strong> the slide describes exclusion from certain JPMorgan ESG indices following a low ESG score.</p>\n<p>Conceptual pathway:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"ESG performance → index eligibility → capital-market implications\"><span>ESG performance</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>index eligibility</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>capital-market implications</span></div>\n<p><strong>Forced-labour allegations involving Malaysian sectors:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$S\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{trade restriction}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{revenue/operations}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Sapura Energy executive remuneration issue:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$G\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{shareholder dissatisfaction}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{director accountability}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Serba Dinamik financial-statement case:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$G\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{reporting credibility}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{regulatory/reputation/market consequences}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Tesla ESG-index example in the slide:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$S/G concerns\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{index decision}$$</div>\n<p>The lesson is not:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"ESG causes scandals.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The lesson is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Different ESG weaknesses transmit into different enterprise risks.</span></div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4n-s28\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">28. Greenwashing: one of the most important concepts in this topic</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter defines the basic problem clearly: some companies seek an environmentally friendly image without making serious underlying changes.</p>\n<p>So:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Greenwashing}\n=\n\\text{Sustainability claim} &gt; \\text{Sustainability reality}\n}$$</div>\n<p>Conceptually, imagine:</p>\n<p>Actual environmental improvement:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$10$$</div>\n<p>Marketing impression:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$90$$</div>\n<p>The gap:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$90-10=80$$</div>\n<p>is where credibility risk lives.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4n-s29\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">29. Why greenwashing is a governance problem as much as an environmental problem</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"t4_greenwash_detector\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Claim versus evidence</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Set how bold the public claim is, add evidence, and read the gap.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Suppose marketing publishes:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Carbon neutral product.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>But nobody has properly verified the claim.</p>\n<p>At first glance this is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$E$$</div>\n<p>But ask deeper questions.</p>\n<p>Who approved the claim?</p>\n<p>Was evidence checked?</p>\n<p>Were controls adequate?</p>\n<p>Was management intentionally misleading stakeholders?</p>\n<p>Those are:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$G$$</div>\n<p>questions.</p>\n<p>Then consumers discover the problem.</p>\n<p>Now:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Reputation Risk}$$</div>\n<p>Regulators investigate.</p>\n<p>Now:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Compliance/Legal Risk}$$</div>\n<p>Sales may fall.</p>\n<p>Now:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Business Risk}$$</div>\n<p>So one greenwashing incident can produce:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{E+G+\\text{Reputation}+\\text{Legal}+\\text{Financial}}$$</div>\n<p>That is why ESG issues fit naturally within ERM.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4n-s30\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">30. The Volkswagen example shows how environmental misconduct becomes financial loss</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter uses Volkswagen's emissions controversy as a greenwashing-related example and notes very large fines, penalties and restitution by 2018.</p>\n<p>The useful causal analysis is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single\"><span>Environmental claim/control failure</span></div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Governance/ethical failure}$$</div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Regulatory discovery}$$</div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Penalties}$$</div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Reputation damage}$$</div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Financial consequence</span></div>\n<p>Again:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$E/G\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{financial outcome}$$</div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4n-s31\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">31. Tlou Energy illustrates another greenwashing pathway</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter states that Australia's ASIC fined Tlou Energy for factually incorrect statements concerning environmental credentials.</p>\n<p>This illustrates a basic rule:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Sustainability disclosure itself can create regulatory risk</span></div>\n<p>If the underlying measurement is weak, exaggeration can transform what management thought was a marketing advantage into:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{legal}\n+\n\\text{reputational}\n+\n\\text{financial}$$</div>\n<p>risk.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4n-s32\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">32. Coca-Cola example: ESG claims can be challenged against operational reality</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter presents criticism and litigation surrounding sustainability marketing and plastic waste.</p>\n<p>The higher-level question is:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Are public sustainability claims consistent with the firm's actual operating footprint?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That gives you a useful test:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Claim}\n\\overset{?}{=}\n\\text{Evidence}\n}$$</div>\n<p>If not:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Greenwashing Risk} \\uparrow$$</div>\n<p>This is exactly why credible metrics and reporting controls matter.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4n-s33\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">33. What does ESG risk management actually do?</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter says ESG risk management broadens management beyond conventional business and financial risks and helps tailor the business model to manage environmental, social and governance risks.</p>\n<p>But don't imagine this means creating a completely independent second ERM department.</p>\n<p>A more coherent architecture is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{ERM}\n\\supset\n\\text{Material ESG Risks}\n}$$</div>\n<p>You already have:</p>\n<p>strategic risk,</p>\n<p>operational risk,</p>\n<p>market risk,</p>\n<p>reputation risk,</p>\n<p>compliance risk,</p>\n<p>liquidity risk.</p>\n<p>ESG provides additional <strong>risk drivers and indicators</strong> that should feed those processes.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4n-s34\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">34. ESG risk management should therefore follow a familiar ERM cycle</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Take climate risk.</p>\n<p>First define the objective:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single\"><span>Maintain reliable production</span></div>\n<p>Then identify ESG driver:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{extreme weather}$$</div>\n<p>Assess exposure:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{factory in flood-prone area}$$</div>\n<p>Assess likelihood/severity.</p>\n<p>Develop controls:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{drainage}\n+\n\\text{business continuity}\n+\n\\text{alternate site}\n+\n\\text{insurance}$$</div>\n<p>Monitor indicators:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{weather}\n+\n\\text{flood levels}\n+\n\\text{asset exposure}$$</div>\n<p>Report upward.</p>\n<p>That is not exotic.</p>\n<p>It is simply <strong>risk management applied to sustainability-related uncertainty</strong>.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4n-s35\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">35. The value of ESG risk management is largely about earlier detection</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"t4_kri_lead\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Early warning from ESG KRIs</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Move the alert threshold on a near-miss KRI and see how much warning you get before the accident.</figcaption></figure></div><p>The chapter says ESG RM can provide near-real-time monitoring, early warning, proactive management, employee incentives, benchmarking and objective business information.</p>\n<p>The phrase <strong>early warning system</strong> matters.</p>\n<p>Compare:</p>\n<h4>Reactive management</h4>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Scandal}$$</div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{News headlines}$$</div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Management reacts}$$</div>\n<p>versus:</p>\n<h4>Proactive management</h4>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{KRI deteriorates}$$</div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Management investigates}$$</div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Control introduced}$$</div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Major incident avoided}$$</div>\n<p>Risk management works best before the front page of the newspaper helpfully explains your control failure to you.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4n-s36\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">36. Think in terms of ESG KRIs</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>This is an intuitive extension of the chapter's early-warning idea.</p>\n<p>For Environmental risks, possible indicators might include:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{energy intensity}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{GHG emissions}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{water consumption}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{waste incidents}$$</div>\n<p>For Social risks:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{injury rate}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{staff turnover}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{supplier labour violations}$$</div>\n<p>For Governance:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{control breaches}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{whistleblower complaints}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-flow single\"><span>board independence indicators</span></div>\n<p>The precise KRI should depend on the company's material exposures.</p>\n<p>That's how ESG becomes operational rather than decorative.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4n-s37\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">37. Sustainability reporting and ESG risk management are related but not identical</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t4_reporting_vs_rm\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>A credible report sits on governance, measurement and controls; a pretty one can sit on nothing.</figcaption></figure></div><p>This distinction matters.</p>\n<p>Reporting asks:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>What do we disclose?</span></div>\n<p>Risk management asks:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>What do we do about the risk?</span></div>\n<p>A company could produce an excellent-looking sustainability report while having terrible risk controls.</p>\n<p>Conversely, it could have strong internal sustainability controls but weak external reporting.</p>\n<p>Ideally:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Risk Management → Reliable Data → Reporting\"><span>Risk Management</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Reliable Data</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Reporting</span></div>\n<p>not:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Marketing Department → Pretty Report\"><span>Marketing Department</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Pretty Report</span></div>\n<p>with no operating substance underneath.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4n-s38\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">38. This explains why sustainability reporting requires controls</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose the company reports:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$100,000\\text{ tCO}_2e$$</div>\n<p>of emissions.</p>\n<p>Who collected the data?</p>\n<p>What boundaries were used?</p>\n<p>Were subsidiaries included?</p>\n<p>Was the calculation reviewed?</p>\n<p>Could management manipulate the number?</p>\n<p>Those are Topic 3 governance questions applied to Topic 4.</p>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{ESG Reporting Quality}\n=\n\\text{Measurement}\n+\n\\text{Controls}\n+\n\\text{Governance}\n}$$</div>\n<p>not merely graphic design.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4n-s39\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">39. Green finance vs green investing</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter distinguishes them.</p>\n<p>Green finance includes financing related to environmentally focused activities, including climate finance, while the slide says it excludes the wider social/economic dimensions.</p>\n<p>Green investing focuses investment toward companies or projects associated with favourable environmental practices such as natural-resource conservation and pollution reduction.</p>\n<p>An intuitive distinction:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Green Finance} \\approx \\text{How environmentally beneficial activity is financed}}$$</div>\n<p>while:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Green Investing} \\approx \\text{Where investors allocate capital for environmental outcomes/exposure}}$$</div>\n<p>They overlap but the perspective differs.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4n-s40\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">40. Sustainable finance creates an important incentive mechanism</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Imagine Company A has strong sustainability practices.</p>\n<p>Investors perceive:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single\"><span>lower unmanaged ESG exposure</span></div>\n<p>That may improve its attractiveness to certain investors or financing programmes.</p>\n<p>Company B has serious unresolved environmental liabilities.</p>\n<p>Investors may demand:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single\"><span>higher expected compensation</span></div>\n<p>or avoid it.</p>\n<p>So capital markets can create:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow boxed\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Financial incentives → Corporate ESG behaviour\"><span>Financial incentives</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Corporate ESG behaviour</span></div>\n<p>This helps explain why ESG moved from the sustainability department into CFO, investor-relations and board discussions.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4n-s41\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">41. Malaysia's sustainability context</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t4_net_zero\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Net zero = gross emissions minus removals equals zero; it needs a measured baseline.</figcaption></figure></div><p>The chapter presents Malaysia's sustainability development through several examples.</p>\n<p>One slide states that Malaysia announced a carbon-neutrality target in 2021 and explains net zero as balancing greenhouse-gas emissions produced with emissions removed from the atmosphere.</p>\n<p>For this course, the conceptual distinction is more important than memorising slogans:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Gross Emissions}\n-\n\\text{Removal/Absorption}\n=\n\\text{Net Emissions}$$</div>\n<p>Net zero aims approximately for:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Net Emissions}=0}$$</div>\n<p>That does <strong>not</strong> necessarily mean:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Gross Emissions}=0$$</div>\n<p>Very different idea.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4n-s42\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">42. Corporate examples show that firms map sustainability differently</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter uses IOI Group and PETRONAS examples.</p>\n<p>The IOI slide shows the company mapping selected SDGs to its own sustainability priorities rather than claiming every SDG is equally relevant.</p>\n<p>That is sensible.</p>\n<p>A company should ask:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Which SDGs are actually relevant to our operations and impacts?</span></div>\n<p>rather than plastering 17 colourful icons across an annual report and calling the problem solved.</p>\n<p>PETRONAS is shown using four sustainability lenses, including continued value creation, positive social impact, safeguarding the environment and responsible governance.</p>\n<p>This reinforces:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Corporate Sustainability}\n=\n\\text{business-specific translation of broad sustainability principles}\n}$$</div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4n-s43\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">43. This is why copying another company's ESG strategy is weak</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose PETRONAS has major:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{GHG}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{energy transition}$$</div>\n<p>issues.</p>\n<p>A bank may instead have material concerns around:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{governance}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{data privacy}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{financed activities}$$</div>\n<p>A technology company might have:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{privacy}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{human capital}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{data-centre energy}$$</div>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>ESG strategy should be tailored to the firm&#x27;s actual exposure.</span></div>\n<p>Exactly like ISO 31000's idea of tailored risk management from Topic 3.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4n-s44\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">44. Malaysia's ESG practices</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter describes Malaysian initiatives including FTSE4Good ratings, alignment with the SDGs, climate-related collaboration between financial regulators, awareness efforts, incentives and government-private-sector initiatives.</p>\n<p>What does this tell you conceptually?</p>\n<p>ESG adoption is not driven by one single force.</p>\n<p>It can come from:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Regulation}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Stock exchanges}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Investors}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Lenders}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Customers}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Government}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Supply-chain requirements}$$</div>\n<p>This is why ESG becomes an enterprise issue rather than a niche department.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4n-s45\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">45. FTSE4Good Bursa Malaysia: don't misunderstand what an ESG index is doing</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter says the FTSE4Good Bursa Malaysia Index draws constituents from the FTSE Bursa Malaysia EMAS universe and describes it as a local stock-market measure relating to ESG compliance/performance.</p>\n<p>An index does not magically make a company sustainable.</p>\n<p>Instead, think:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Index}\n=\n\\text{classification/benchmark/filter based on defined criteria}\n}$$</div>\n<p>A company might want inclusion because it can:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>signal ESG performance,</li>\n<li>increase visibility,</li>\n<li>align with certain investors.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>But index inclusion should not become the programme's only purpose.</p>\n<p>Otherwise management may optimise for:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{index score}$$</div>\n<p>instead of:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single\"><span>underlying risk reduction and sustainable value</span></div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4n-s46\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">46. Sustainability vs ESG metrics - probably one of the most examinable distinctions</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Page 45 states:</p>\n<p><strong>Sustainability</strong> concerns meeting present needs without compromising future generations.</p>\n<p><strong>ESG metrics</strong> measure how sustainable a company's performance is across effects on prosperity, planet and people, helping indicate the \"true value\" created by the organisation.</p>\n<p>This gives a clean distinction:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Sustainability} = \\text{concept / desired state}}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{ESG} = \\text{organisational dimensions}}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{ESG metrics} = \\text{measurement}}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{SDGs} = \\text{global goals}}$$</div>\n<p>This is worth understanding extremely well.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4n-s47\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">47. A thermometer analogy</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t4_concepts_ladder\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Goal, global targets, business translation, dimensions, measurement and external outcome.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Think of:</p>\n<p><strong>Sustainability</strong> as health.</p>\n<p><strong>SDGs</strong> as public-health objectives.</p>\n<p><strong>ESG practices</strong> as what the patient does.</p>\n<p><strong>ESG metrics</strong> as blood pressure, cholesterol and temperature.</p>\n<p>The measurement is useful.</p>\n<p>But:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Thermometer} \\neq \\text{Health}$$</div>\n<p>Similarly:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{ESG score} \\neq \\text{Sustainability itself}}$$</div>\n<p>A metric is only an indicator.</p>\n<p>That is why blindly chasing scores can create distorted behaviour.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4n-s48\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">48. The chapter's ASEAN comparison should be interpreted cautiously</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Page 41 visually compares ESG risk exposure and management scores across ASEAN-6 and reference regions.</p>\n<p>The important analytical point is not to obsess over one bar height.</p>\n<p>The framework is distinguishing:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Exposure to ESG Risk</span></div>\n<p>from:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Ability to Manage ESG Risk</span></div>\n<p>These are different.</p>\n<p>Company A could face huge ESG exposure but manage it exceptionally well.</p>\n<p>Company B could face moderate exposure but manage it terribly.</p>\n<p>So:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Risk Exposure}\n\\neq\n\\text{Risk Management Quality}\n}$$</div>\n<p>That's a valuable concept far beyond ESG.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4n-s49\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">49. The sustainable bond and sukuk chart is really about capital-market response</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Page 42 shows strong growth in ASEAN-6 sustainable bonds and sukuk over the period shown.</p>\n<p>You do not need to treat this merely as a graph to memorise.</p>\n<p>The economic meaning is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Sustainability goals}$$</div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{need for financing}$$</div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single\"><span>financial instruments develop</span></div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{capital markets participate}$$</div>\n<p>Therefore sustainability increasingly enters:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>corporate finance</span></div>\n<p>not just environmental management.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4n-s50\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">50. A difficult integrated case</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose a Malaysian listed manufacturer wants:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{FTSE4Good inclusion}$$</div>\n<p>and:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single\"><span>access to sustainable financing</span></div>\n<p>Management proposes publishing a glossy sustainability report.</p>\n<p>But the firm has:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single\"><span>no reliable emissions baseline</span></div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{no supplier assessment}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-flow single\"><span>no board-level ESG oversight</span></div>\n<p>What should you say?</p>\n<p>A weak answer:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Publish the sustainability report and communicate ESG initiatives.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That is exactly how organisations wander into greenwashing.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4n-s51\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">51. Start with governance, not marketing</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The company first needs:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Governance</span></div>\n<p>Who owns ESG?</p>\n<p>Who reports to the board?</p>\n<p>Who approves targets?</p>\n<p>Who checks data?</p>\n<p>Without this:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{ESG programme}$$</div>\n<p>has no accountability.</p>\n<p>So Topic 3 comes first.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4n-s52\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">52. Then determine material ESG risks</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>For a manufacturer these might include, depending on actual operations:</p>\n<p>Environmental:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{energy}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{emissions}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{water}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{waste}$$</div>\n<p>Social:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{worker safety}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{labour standards}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{supply-chain practices}$$</div>\n<p>Governance:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{board oversight}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{ethics}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{controls}$$</div>\n<p>Don't blindly collect every ESG issue on Earth.</p>\n<p>Use:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>materiality + business exposure</span></div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4n-s53\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">53. Establish baselines</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"t4_net_zero_calc\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Net zero arithmetic</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Combine real cuts and bought offsets: &quot;net zero&quot; can be reached while gross emissions barely move.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Suppose the company wants:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Reduce emissions 40%.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Forty percent of what?</p>\n<p>Without a baseline:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Target}$$</div>\n<p>has no denominator.</p>\n<p>So first measure:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$E_0 = \\text{Baseline emissions}$$</div>\n<p>Then if target reduction is 40%:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$E_{target}=E_0(1-0.40)$$</div>\n<p>Now management has something measurable.</p>\n<p>This is why data integrity comes before grand targets.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4n-s54\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">54. Assess suppliers</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>If your own factory has beautiful labour conditions but your critical supplier uses abusive practices:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Supply-chain ESG exposure}$$</div>\n<p>still exists.</p>\n<p>Recall the Malaysian forced-labour example from the chapter.</p>\n<p>Risk does not stop at the legal boundary of the company.</p>\n<p>Therefore supplier due diligence belongs inside ESG risk management.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4n-s55\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">55. Create controls and KRIs</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>For emissions:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{GHG intensity}$$</div>\n<p>For safety:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{incident rate}$$</div>\n<p>For suppliers:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\%\\text{ suppliers assessed}$$</div>\n<p>For governance:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single\"><span>ESG issues escalated to board</span></div>\n<p>Now ESG becomes monitorable.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4n-s56\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">56. Only then report</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t4_correct_order\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Governance first, disclosure last: the reverse order is how firms wander into greenwashing.</figcaption></figure></div><p>The correct chain is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow boxed\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Governance → Materiality → Data → Baseline → Targets → Controls → Performance → Disclosure\"><span>Governance</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Materiality</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Data</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Baseline</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Targets</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Controls</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Performance</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Disclosure</span></div>\n<p>Not:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow boxed\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Marketing agency → green photos → 80-page PDF\"><span>Marketing agency</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>green photos</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>80-page PDF</span></div>\n<p>The second approach may look prettier. Unfortunately, trees in annual reports do not sequester governance failures.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4n-s57\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">57. Why ESG index inclusion should be an outcome</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"t4_score_vs_reality\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Chasing the rating</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Split the ESG budget between polishing the score and changing operations.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Suppose management's objective is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Get ESG rating}$$</div>\n<p>Then incentives become:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Do whatever improves rating}$$</div>\n<p>That may encourage superficial metric optimisation.</p>\n<p>Better objective:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Manage material sustainability risks and create resilient long-term value</span></div>\n<p>If done credibly:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Better ESG disclosure}$$</div>\n<p>and potentially:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single\"><span>better ratings/index eligibility/financing access</span></div>\n<p>may follow.</p>\n<p>So:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow boxed\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Good ESG Performance → Rating\"><span>Good ESG Performance</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Rating</span></div>\n<p>not:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow boxed\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Rating → pretend ESG performance\"><span>Rating</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>pretend ESG performance</span></div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4n-s58\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">58. A second difficult case</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Imagine a food manufacturer announces:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"We will become carbon neutral.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>At the same time:</p>\n<p>the company cannot reliably measure emissions,</p>\n<p>its largest supplier is accused of forced labour,</p>\n<p>the board has no sustainability committee or oversight mechanism,</p>\n<p>and executive bonuses depend only on short-term EBITDA.</p>\n<p>Analyse this.</p>\n<p>Environmental problem:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{unverified emissions target}$$</div>\n<p>Social problem:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single\"><span>supply-chain labour exposure</span></div>\n<p>Governance problem:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{weak oversight + incentives}$$</div>\n<p>Greenwashing risk:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single\"><span>public claim exceeds evidence</span></div>\n<p>ERM problem:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single\"><span>ESG risks are not embedded into decision-making</span></div>\n<p>Potential consequences:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{regulatory}\n+\n\\text{reputational}\n+\n\\text{supply disruption}\n+\n\\text{capital-market}\n+\n\\text{financial}$$</div>\n<p>Now you're analysing rather than labelling.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4n-s59\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">59. Evaluate whether the carbon-neutral pledge itself is good</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>You should not say:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Yes, because sustainability is good.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Nor:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>No, because it is expensive.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Instead ask:</p>\n<p>Does the company know its baseline?</p>\n<p>Is the target measurable?</p>\n<p>Is the pathway credible?</p>\n<p>Who owns it?</p>\n<p>What investment is required?</p>\n<p>What are the risks?</p>\n<p>Are suppliers included where relevant?</p>\n<p>What happens if targets are missed?</p>\n<p>How will performance be reported?</p>\n<p>That is Bloom's <strong>Evaluate</strong> level.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4n-s60\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">60. Bloom's Taxonomy applied to Topic 4</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t4_bloom_ladder\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Marks rise as the question moves from defining ESG to designing an ESG risk programme.</figcaption></figure></div><p>At <strong>Remember</strong>, the lecturer might ask you to define ESG or identify the 3Ps.</p>\n<p>At <strong>Understand</strong>, you might explain why sustainability goes beyond environmental protection.</p>\n<p>At <strong>Apply</strong>, you might classify a forced-labour allegation under Social ESG and explain the business consequences.</p>\n<p>At <strong>Analyse</strong>, you might be given a pollution scandal and asked to trace environmental, governance, reputation, compliance and financial consequences.</p>\n<p>At <strong>Evaluate</strong>, you might assess whether a company's net-zero strategy is credible based on governance, measurement, controls and targets.</p>\n<p>At <strong>Create</strong>, you might design an ESG risk-management programme for a listed company.</p>\n<p>At that highest level your architecture would look something like:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Business Objectives</span></div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Material ESG Issues</span></div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Risk Assessment</span></div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Governance + Risk Owners</span></div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Baselines + Metrics + KRIs</span></div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Targets + Controls + Responses</span></div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Monitoring</span></div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Disclosure</span></div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Review + Improvement</span></div>\n<p>That is actual ESG risk management.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4n-s61\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">61. The most important distinctions to master</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>If you remember nothing else, keep these separate:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Concept</th>\n<th>Best way to think about it</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Sustainability</td>\n<td>Long-term ability to meet present needs without undermining future needs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sustainable Development</td>\n<td>Development that balances economic, environmental and social needs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Triple Bottom Line</td>\n<td>People + Planet + Profit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SDGs</td>\n<td>Global sustainable-development goals</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Corporate Sustainability</td>\n<td>Sustainability translated into business strategy and operations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ESG</td>\n<td>Environmental, Social and Governance dimensions relevant to organisations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ESG metrics</td>\n<td>Indicators used to measure ESG performance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ESG risk</td>\n<td>Material E/S/G condition that may affect organisational/investment value</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ESG Risk Management</td>\n<td>Identifying, assessing, controlling and monitoring material ESG risks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Greenwashing</td>\n<td>Sustainability claims that exceed substantive performance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ESG rating/index</td>\n<td>External measurement/classification outcome, not sustainability itself</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>That table is probably the single most useful revision device for this topic.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4n-s62\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">62. How Topic 4 connects all four chapters</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t4_concept_map\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>From the idea of sustainability to enterprise value, and how E, S and G migrate into familiar risks.</figcaption></figure></div><p>We can now build the whole course progression.</p>\n<h4>Topic 1</h4>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Risk = uncertainty affecting objectives</span></div>\n<h4>Topic 2</h4>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Manage risks holistically across the enterprise</span></div>\n<h4>Topic 3</h4>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Govern, control and standardise how risk is managed</span></div>\n<h4>Topic 4</h4>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Expand the risk horizon to environmental, social and governance factors affecting long-term value</span></div>\n<p>Notice Topic 4 does <strong>not replace ERM</strong>.</p>\n<p>It extends it.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t4n-s63\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">63. The deepest lesson from Topic 4</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>A surface-level understanding says:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>ESG is about companies being environmentally friendly and socially responsible.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That's not enough for this course.</p>\n<p>A stronger understanding is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>ESG provides a structured way to identify environmental, social and governance conditions that may affect the sustainability and long-term value of an organisation.</span></div>\n<p>The advanced ERM interpretation is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>ESG problems rarely remain \"ESG problems.\"</span></div>\n<p>They migrate.</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Environmental → Operational → Financial\"><span>Environmental</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Operational</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Financial</span></div>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Social → Supply Chain → Reputation → Revenue\"><span>Social</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Supply Chain</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Reputation</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Revenue</span></div>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Governance → Fraud → Regulation → Capital Market\"><span>Governance</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Fraud</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Regulation</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Capital Market</span></div>\n<p>And that brings you directly back to enterprise risk management.</p>\n<p>The best question to ask in any ESG case is therefore not merely:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>\"Is this E, S or G?\"</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Ask:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>\"What is the underlying ESG issue, which objective does it threaten, how does the risk transmit through the business, who is accountable for it, what indicators would warn us early, what controls would reduce it, and what happens to enterprise value if management gets it wrong?\"</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>If you can answer that for an unfamiliar company, you understand Topic 4 at the <strong>Analyse-Evaluate-Create</strong> levels rather than merely recognising ESG terminology.</p></div></details></div></details>");
